BUILD_ONLY: ghcr.io/embodied-agent-interface-v2-internal/eai-robolab:<images/robolab/VERSION> is never pushed to a
registry (docs/PROTOCOL.md §2.4, D5). Every evaluation host builds it locally; the RoboLab tasks reference that tag and
Harbor finds it in the local Docker image store.

Why
  The image contains NVIDIA Isaac Sim 5.1 / Omniverse Kit and the Isaac Lab 2.3.2 pip wheels (installed from
  pypi.nvidia.com). NVIDIA licenses them for use, not for redistribution, so the built image may not be published.
  RoboLab itself (github.com/NVlabs/RoboLab v0.3.1, code and its ~6 GB LFS asset library) is Apache-2.0.

What the builder accepts
  - the NVIDIA Isaac Sim / Omniverse license agreement (the image sets OMNI_KIT_ACCEPT_EULA=YES and ACCEPT_EULA=Y, and
    every container that runs it accepts it again at launch);
  - NVIDIA's terms for the isaacsim / isaaclab wheels on pypi.nvidia.com;
  - RoboLab's Apache-2.0 licence (its assets are baked in, as RoboLab's own docker/Dockerfile does).
  No account, key or token is needed: everything is downloaded anonymously.

Data
  None beyond the image: the RoboLab asset library is baked in; nothing is mounted at run time. The frozen task
  instances live in the task directories.

Host
  Linux x86_64, Docker with the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, an RTX-class NVIDIA GPU with a driver Isaac Sim 5.1 supports
  (Isaac renders with RTX even headless; PhysX runs on the CPU). Checked on RTX 4090 (laptop and desktop) and RTX PRO
  6000. Build: ~40 GB of free disk, network access to github.com (+ its LFS), astral.sh, pypi.org and pypi.nvidia.com,
  about 45-60 min. Run: one Isaac simulator per 32 GB of host RAM (the standard sidecar and its replays run one at a time).

Build (from the repository root)
  docker build -f images/robolab/Dockerfile -t ghcr.io/embodied-agent-interface-v2-internal/eai-robolab:$(cat images/robolab/VERSION) .
  (scripts/build_images.sh builds every images/<sim>/ with its own naming; until it names images eai-<sim>, use the
  command above.) The build context is the repository root: the Dockerfile copies images/robolab/uv.lock, which pins
  every Python package (torch 2.7.0+cu128, numpy 1.26.0, isaacsim 5.1.0.0, isaaclab 2.3.2.post1).

Check a build
  The frozen instances must rebuild to their recorded state hashes, and a fresh-process replay of a reference trajectory
  must end in its recorded hash. With a task directory at hand:
    docker run --rm --gpus all --shm-size 8g -v $PWD/tasks/robolab-banana-in-bowl-i00-privileged:/t \
      ghcr.io/embodied-agent-interface-v2-internal/eai-robolab:$(cat images/robolab/VERSION) bash -c \
      "cd /tmp && python /t/environment/rcb_robolab.py run-oracle --task banana_in_bowl --instance /t/environment/instance.json \
         --code /t/solution/oracle_banana_in_bowl.py --out /tmp/t.npz && \
       python /t/environment/rcb_robolab.py replay --task banana_in_bowl --instance /t/environment/instance.json --traj /tmp/t.npz"
  expects instance_exact true (instance sha 7c211bc3fe6d80cd), success true, and the same final state_sha from both
  commands. Compare the two commands, not hosts: the final hash can differ between hosts (two values seen on ours,
  61afa384566be3a5 and 902e40deb3d42ed0, both successes). Or run `harbor run -p tasks/robolab-banana-in-bowl-i00-privileged -a oracle`.

On a CI machine (maintainers)
  A self-hosted runner with such a GPU builds the image with the command above when images/robolab/ or VERSION changes,
  runs the check, and keeps the image in its local Docker store (or in a registry the organization keeps private), never
  on a public registry. Hosts of the same organization may copy a built image among themselves
  (docker save ... | zstd, docker load); a new VERSION means a rebuild on every host.
